{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "021L", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$S$", "MATH_0002": "$\\Sch_{fppf}$", "MATH_0003": "$S$", "MATH_0004": "$\\Sch_{fppf}$", "MATH_0005": "$(\\Sch/S)_{fppf}$", "MATH_0006": "$(\\textit{Aff}/S)_{fppf}$", "MATH_0007": "$\\Sch$", "MATH_0008": "$(\\Sch/S)_{fppf}$", "MATH_0009": "$S/S$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-fibre-products-fppf}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:caf3fe0e9c9382d91e03a24c9f9fc9aa590157bdf49864f768b6ce297498f3b5", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:4887f7a45ab65325b51fd4f55da90c61c566b3cfd0428a79d872602ebcbf5a3b", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a scheme. Let <MATH_0002> be a big fppf\nsite containing <MATH_0003>. The underlying categories of the sites\n<MATH_0004>, <MATH_0005>,\nand <MATH_0006> have fibre products.\nIn each case the obvious functor into the category <MATH_0007> of\nall schemes commutes with taking fibre products. The category\n<MATH_0008> has a final object, namely <MATH_0009>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:cb750f25afc7e8addb85c89ac2ad7c5ab2e98d974ac0fd9f135d575f24d3143c", "unit_id": "tag:021U:statement"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "021L", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\Sch_{fppf}$", "MATH_0002": "$U \\to S$", "MATH_0003": "$V \\to U$", "MATH_0004": "$W \\to U$", "MATH_0005": "$U, V, W \\in \\Ob(\\Sch_{fppf})$", "MATH_0006": "$V \\times_U W$", "MATH_0007": "$\\Sch_{fppf}$", "MATH_0008": "$\\Sch$", "MATH_0009": "$V/S$", "MATH_0010": "$W/S$", "MATH_0011": "$U/S$", "MATH_0012": "$S$", "MATH_0013": "$(\\Sch/S)_{fppf}$", "MATH_0014": "$(\\Sch/S)_{fppf}$", "MATH_0015": "$U, V, W$", "MATH_0016": "$V \\times_U W$", "MATH_0017": "$(\\textit{Aff}/S)_{fppf}$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{sets-lemma-what-is-in-it}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:4cf744dad6420c6473f570be4a0fca98a9c383f66297a1216ff3aab90ff1fbba", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:3d9398ace99746c1f9b94deb946af4e2e2ba5c45fb79697cf991264fb94ae3a4", "source_text": "For <MATH_0001> it is true by construction, see\nSets, Lemma <REF_0001>.\nSuppose we have <MATH_0002>, <MATH_0003>, <MATH_0004> morphisms\nof schemes with <MATH_0005>.\nThe fibre product <MATH_0006> in <MATH_0007>\nis a fibre product in <MATH_0008> and\nis the fibre product of <MATH_0009> with <MATH_0010> over <MATH_0011> in\nthe category of all schemes over <MATH_0012>, and hence also a\nfibre product in <MATH_0013>.\nThis proves the result for <MATH_0014>.\nIf <MATH_0015> are affine, so is <MATH_0016> and hence the\nresult for <MATH_0017>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:f41aebdd466242fd28cc4e09fdbc00ed406926d2acaf8b26b8b7904821885dac", "unit_id": "tag:021U:proof"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "021L", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:6945887321fe30912185bfe21c93363c5ca2e2a223ff071a1d0afa8ad2cf0e45", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nNext, we check that the big affine site defines the same\ntopos as the big site.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:78c6b1773b894b6b6a83aad54cff4dcbe080120f2b8498c883a3a9768e16ad14", "unit_id": "tag:021L:prose-0006"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "021L", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$S$", "MATH_0002": "$\\Sch_{fppf}$", "MATH_0003": "$S$", "MATH_0004": "$(\\textit{Aff}/S)_{fppf} \\to (\\Sch/S)_{fppf}$", "MATH_0005": "$\\Sh((\\textit{Aff}/S)_{fppf})$", "MATH_0006": "$\\Sh((\\Sch/S)_{fppf})$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-affine-big-site-fppf}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:cbb93e3173ebdc7647df55fc10066b290047b5fea3551418c7e61ae122fad1a7", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:8776cb1c32e68e3789a6c12b87f25820bbeef1d3d085b81c0ba464a1dd4b8307", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a scheme. Let <MATH_0002> be a big fppf\nsite containing <MATH_0003>.\nThe functor <MATH_0004>\nis cocontinuous and induces an equivalence of topoi from\n<MATH_0005> to\n<MATH_0006>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:2952e77778bbbdf1344808eaf1266ba706f10628fac37502930b9fa40f1792b8", "unit_id": "tag:021V:statement"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "021L", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$u : (\\textit{Aff}/S)_{fppf} \\to (\\Sch/S)_{fppf}$", "MATH_0002": "$T/S$", "MATH_0003": "$T$", "MATH_0004": "$T$", "MATH_0005": "$u$", "MATH_0006": "$u$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{sites-definition-special-cocontinuous-functor}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{sites-lemma-equivalence}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{lemma-fppf-affine}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:3ffd73aad15df0544dfc060f2f8490a7b88fd488ac2ceb01a3bdb753feee5d24", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:0283d068203517251eab0d95ff5603f3717dafeb48e24863ed0c57d10d61fc70", "source_text": "The notion of a special cocontinuous functor is introduced in\nSites, Definition <REF_0001>.\nThus we have to verify assumptions (1) -- (5) of\nSites, Lemma <REF_0002>.\nDenote the inclusion functor\n<MATH_0001>.\nBeing cocontinuous just means that any fppf covering of\n<MATH_0002>, <MATH_0003> affine, can be refined by a standard fppf covering of <MATH_0004>.\nThis is the content of\nLemma <REF_0003>.\nHence (1) holds. We see <MATH_0005> is continuous simply because a standard\nfppf covering is a fppf covering. Hence (2) holds.\nParts (3) and (4) follow immediately from the fact that <MATH_0006> is\nfully faithful. And finally condition (5) follows from the\nfact that every scheme has an affine open covering.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:22bc0603d136fd589b699c7cae9fa2ae562005edae4d754d13e0969d9d797ce3", "unit_id": "tag:021V:proof"}
